# PRIVATE companion to denylist.txt. GITIGNORED AND NEVER EXPORTED.
#
# Same format: one extended-regex per line, '#' comments and blank lines ignored.
# check-export-clean.sh reads this file AND denylist.txt as one list and fails the
# export on any hit.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS SEPARATELY. denylist.txt ships. A pattern that names a third
# party's schema would therefore publish, in the public repository, the very string the
# pattern exists to keep out of it -- the same reason Scripts/export/exclude.txt names
# no private path and the repository's own notes carry the list instead.
#
# WHAT GOES HERE: a name that is somebody ELSE'S to disclose. Table, package, schema and
# product names carried over from customer work.
#
# WHAT DOES NOT: anything of ours -- our test schema, the access code, our own host names.
# Those stay in the public denylist, where they cost nothing and keep it self-explanatory.
#
# THIS TEMPLATE SHIPS, so it spells none of them either. A denied name written here matches
# its own pattern and fails every future export. That happened TWICE while this file was
# being written: once in check-export-clean.sh, and once here.
#
# When a hit is legitimate, FIX THE FILE -- do not loosen a pattern here. That rule is
# the whole value of a gate that refuses rather than a scrubber that rewrites.

# --- third-party schema and table names -------------------------------------
# Carried over from customer work. Structure only, never data -- but a table layout is
# still that organisation's, and a push cannot be retracted.
# <one extended-regex per line; see the private notes for the actual names>
# <one extended-regex per line; see the private notes for the actual names>
# --- third-party schema and table names -------------------------------------
# One extended-regex per line. The actual names are NOT in this template, for the same
# reason they are not in denylist.txt: a template ships too.
#
# Take them from the repository's own private notes, which carry the list and the
# reasoning for it. Write each as a character class ([Ll][Ss]...) so the gate's `grep -E`
# matches whatever case the name appears in.
#
# A KNOWN, ACCEPTED EXCEPTION, so nobody adds it here and wonders why the export breaks:
# the BFILE FILENAME in Scripts/provisioning/CheckProvisioning.java. That script ships on
# purpose -- it asks the DATABASE which objects a config names, never reads sql/, and is
# the natural starting point for a contributor building their own schema. A filename is
# not a schema layout. Add a pattern for it and every export fails.
